  "worked_solution": "We wish to express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{515}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle $\\theta ={515}^{∘}.$\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{515}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the corresponding coterminal angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$ We do this by subtracting integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get our desired angle.\n\n$$\n{515}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}={155}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{515}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{155}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253971/assets/515c33e6a1145a67d9a60e929fe1289d7f5931751ba88920a09ea3cbeaf9e020.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute the reference angle. Since $\\theta ={155}^{∘}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{155}^{∘}={25}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Determine the correct sign. The sine function is positive $(+)$ in the second quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{515}^{∘}=+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{515}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}.\n$$",
